-- title:
--   The place t=a differs from the place at infinity
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ be a field and let $a \in K$. Two places of the rational function field $\mathrm{RatFunc}\,K$ over $K$ are compared, a place being recorded by the structure `Place` as a valuation subring of the ambient field that contains the image of $K$ under the structure map, is not the whole field, and is a principal ideal ring. The first is `placeOfPoint K a`, defined as the finite place attached to the irreducible polynomial $X - a$, i.e. the place `Place.ofHeightOneSpectrum` of the height-one prime of $K[X]$ generated by $X - a$. The second is `placeInfty K`, the place whose valuation subring is that of the valuation `RatFunc.inftyValuation K`, i.e. the degree valuation at infinity; the three structure conditions for it are supplied by triviality of that valuation on constants, nontriviality of its value group, and the fact that its valuation ring is a discrete valuation ring, hence a principal ideal ring. The assertion is that these two places are distinct: `placeOfPoint K a ≠ placeInfty K`.
--
--   This is the standard fact that, in the description of the places of $K(t)$ as the monic irreducible polynomials together with the point at infinity, no finite place coincides with the place at infinity; here it is recorded for the places $t = a$ with $a \in K$, the $K$-rational points of the affine line inside $\mathbb{P}^1$. It is used throughout the treatment of divisors and valuations on the rational function field, for instance in the comparison of reductions and orders of divisors and in statements separating the place at infinity from the finite places.
-- source:
